course: dictionary
lesson: 6630
title: What is a noun?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A noun is a naming word. It names a person, place, thing or idea. In «The girl kicked the ball in London», the nouns are girl, ball and London. They tell the reader who, what and where.
explain: Some nouns are special names. «Emma» and «Canada» are proper nouns, and they start with a capital letter. Other nouns are general names, like «dog» or «city». They have no capital unless they start a sentence.
explain: Some nouns name things you can touch, like «table». Others name ideas you cannot touch, like «happiness» or «courage». Countable nouns can be counted, like «sweets». Uncountable nouns are measured, like «sugar» and «water».
fact: A noun is a naming word for a person, place, thing or idea.
fact: «Girl», «Leeds», «table» and «happiness» are all nouns.
fact: A proper noun is the special name of one person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter, like «Emma».
fact: A common noun is a general name, like «dog», and has no capital unless it starts a sentence.
fact: Countable nouns can be counted, like «sweets»; uncountable nouns are measured, like «sugar».
exercise: In «The dog ran to the park», which word is a noun that names a place? | to | the | *park | ran
exercise: Which word is a proper noun, the special name of one place? | jumped | *Canada | city | happy
exercise: Which word is a noun for an idea you cannot touch? | run | *happiness | blue | quickly
test: Which word is an uncountable noun, measured rather than counted? | sweets | *water | dogs | apples
test: In «Emma bought three apples in Leeds», which word is a common noun? | Canada | Emma | *apples | Leeds
exam: Little words change everything: «Circle the word that is NOT a noun» asks the opposite of «Circle the noun», so read the whole question. Which word is NOT a noun? | dog | *quickly | London | courage
